      "id": 1,
      "text": "Roscoe began as a Union Pacific Railroad water stop in the 1870s along the South Platte River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roscoe%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around a double‑track line built by Union Pacific in 1910, bringing automated light signals to the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.keithcountyne.gov/business/history_of_roscoe.php",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Heman “Ben” Chamberlin opened a general store, rental cabins, and a gas station around 1910, making Roscoe a bustling service hub for travelers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/roscoes-nebraska-ghost-town/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s preserved buildings, including Chamberlin’s gas station decorated with Packers memorabilia and Grateful Dead stickers, act as time capsules of small‑town commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/roscoes-nebraska-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Though only about 65 residents remain, Roscoe’s historic architecture tells stories of steam locomotives, Scottish settlers, and resilience in America’s heartland.",
